chapter_notes.1B:
Exposure classification: `limited_exposure_with_choices`

**Reused Evidence**
None.

**New Evidence**

1. Bolsonaro’s official presidential biography records his term as president through 31 December 2022, confirming he was the incumbent ruler for the full period.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Biografia","publisher":"Biblioteca da Presidência da República","publication_date":"undated","url":"https://www.biblioteca.presidencia.gov.br/presidencia/ex-presidentes/bolsonaro/biografia/biografia","claim":"The official presidential biography records Jair Bolsonaro's term in office as 01/01/2019 to 31/12/2022.","locator":"section 'Mandatos' / 'Síntese'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government biography with explicit term dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact match for the 2022 ruler-period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Bolsonaro's office and term.","contrary_evidence":["Biographical pages are favorable by design and omit controversy."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.8","1B.10"]}

2. The Brazilian Constitution gives the president strong executive powers while also constraining movement abroad without congressional permission.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Constituição da República Federativa do Brasil de 1988","publisher":"Planalto","publication_date":"1988-10-05; current consolidated text","url":"https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/constituicao/constituicaocompilado.htm","claim":"Articles 83 and 84 assign the president executive authority, including appointing ministers, directing the federal administration, initiating legislation, promulgating laws, issuing decrees, and vetoing bills, while limiting foreign travel without congressional authorization.","locator":"Art. 83-84","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary constitutional text in force during 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable governing framework throughout 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Defines Bolsonaro's formal authority and constraints in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["Formal powers do not prove how they were used in practice."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"]}

3. Brazil was already a NPT party long before 2022, so Bolsonaro inherited a nonproliferation baseline rather than a weapons-ambition posture.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1998-02-03","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=08000002801205f8","claim":"Brazil acceded to the NPT in 1998.","locator":"participant record: Brazil | Accession | 03/02/1999","source_type":"primary/legal/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN depositary record for treaty accession.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual baseline, not a Bolsonaro-specific initiative.","contrary_evidence":["Accession is a formal commitment, not proof of implementation quality."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.6","1B.10"]}

4. Brazil is a formal party to the Latin American nuclear-weapon-free-zone treaty.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty for the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons in Latin America","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1968-04-26","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=08000002801274b8","claim":"Brazil ratified the Treaty of Tlatelolco, the Latin American nuclear-weapon-free-zone treaty, in 1968.","locator":"participant record: Brazil | Ratification | 26/04/1968","source_type":"primary/legal/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN depositary record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Longstanding baseline in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level treaty posture, not personal authorship by Bolsonaro.","contrary_evidence":["Treaty membership alone does not show active restraint in every year."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.5","1B.6","1B.10"]}

5. Brazil ratified the CTBT and participates in the global verification regime, which is directly relevant to test-ban and monitoring norms.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Brazil ratifies the CTBT","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"1998-07-24","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/resources/for-the-media/press-releases/brazil-ratifies-ctbt","claim":"Brazil deposited its instrument of ratification for the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty in 1998 and was providing monitoring stations to the verification regime.","locator":"first two paragraphs","source_type":"primary/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official CTBTO press release on Brazil's ratification and monitoring contribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable 2022 baseline and verification context.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited state commitment, not Bolsonaro-initiated in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The CTBT was not in force in 2022, so ratification had no full treaty effect."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.9"]}

6. Brazil signed the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons but had not moved to ratification, so the anti-nuclear norm support was real but incomplete.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2017-07-07; current status page","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ShowDetails.aspx?chapter=26&lang=en&mtdsg_no=XXVI-9&src=TREATY","claim":"Brazil appears in the treaty-status table as a signatory to the TPNW, with no ratification shown on the status page excerpt.","locator":"participant table: Brazil | Signature | 20/09/2017","source_type":"primary/legal/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN treaty-status table.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Status remained relevant in 2022 as an unratified signature.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level treaty posture inherited by Bolsonaro.","contrary_evidence":["A signature is weaker than ratification and does not by itself show operational policy change."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

7. The IAEA’s Brazil fact sheet shows the formal safeguards architecture relevant to peaceful-use verification.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Brazil, Federative Republic of","publisher":"IAEA Office of Legal Affairs","publication_date":"2026-03-23; current fact sheet","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=BR","claim":"Brazil's safeguards agreements include the Argentina-Brazil-IAEA quadripartite safeguards agreement and exchange-of-letters arrangements connected to the NPT and Tlatelolco framework.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements table","source_type":"primary/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IAEA country legal-status page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Enduring legal framework relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional baseline rather than Bolsonaro-only action.","contrary_evidence":["The page is legal status, not proof of day-to-day oversight quality."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.6","1B.9","1B.10"]}

8. ABACC’s own description makes clear that the bilateral safeguards system is designed to keep nuclear materials and facilities exclusively peaceful.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"About","publisher":"ABACC","publication_date":"current","url":"https://www.abacc.org.br/en/the-abacc/","claim":"ABACC says its mission is to assure Brazil, Argentina, and the international community that nuclear materials and facilities are used exclusively for peaceful purposes and not diverted to nuclear weapons, and that the agency works with national authorities and the IAEA.","locator":"mission paragraphs on the About page","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official agency self-description of safeguards mission and coordination.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable 2022 institutional context.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional safeguard environment, not direct Bolsonaro conduct.","contrary_evidence":["Self-description is favorable and needs external corroboration for performance claims."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.5","1B.6","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}

9. Brazil’s nuclear safety regulator was created and then operationalized under Bolsonaro’s presidency in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Lei nº 14.222, de 15 de outubro de 2021","publisher":"Planalto","publication_date":"2021-10-15","url":"https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2019-2022/2021/lei/l14222.htm","claim":"The law created the Autoridade Nacional de Segurança Nuclear (ANSN) as an autonomous federal body responsible for monitoring, regulating, and inspecting nuclear safety, radiological protection, and physical security.","locator":"Arts. 1, 2, and 6","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary legal text establishing the regulator and its mandate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant as the institutional framework in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Bolsonaro signed the law, so this is ruler-attributed institutional action.","contrary_evidence":["Creation of a regulator is implementation, not proof of effective supervision in every case."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}

10. The 2022 decree put the ANSN structure into operation and spelled out its safety and physical-protection duties.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Decreto nº 11.142, de 21 de julho de 2022","publisher":"Planalto","publication_date":"2022-07-21","url":"https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2019-2022/2022/decreto/d11142.htm","claim":"The decree implemented ANSN's regimental structure and confirmed that it would monitor, regulate, and inspect nuclear safety, radiological protection, and physical security in Brazil.","locator":"Art. 1-2 and opening text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential decree in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Bolsonaro-era executive action.","contrary_evidence":["This establishes structure, not performance or adequacy of enforcement."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}

11. Bolsonaro also promulgated an amendment to the physical-protection convention for nuclear material and installations in September 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Decreto nº 11.188, de 5 de setembro de 2022","publisher":"Planalto","publication_date":"2022-09-05","url":"https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/_ato2019-2022/2022/decreto/D11188.htm","claim":"The decree promulgated the amendment to the Convention on the Physical Protection of Nuclear Material and Nuclear Installations, a formal step toward stronger physical-security norms.","locator":"decree title and article 3 opening text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential decree implementing an international nuclear-security instrument.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Bolsonaro's presidency.","contrary_evidence":["Promulgation is formal compliance; it does not prove implementation quality at every facility."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.9","1B.10"]}

12. The United States and Brazil explicitly agreed in 2022 to expand cooperation on civil nuclear power and regulation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"United States and Brazil Strengthen Bilateral Cooperation on Energy and Launch a New Public Private Cooperation to Promote Clean Energy","publisher":"U.S. Department of Energy","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://www.energy.gov/ia/articles/united-states-and-brazil-strengthen-bilateral-cooperation-energy-and-launch-new-public","claim":"The two governments agreed to continue and expand cooperation on civil nuclear power and to launch new efforts on civil nuclear regulation and new nuclear power generation.","locator":"bullet list under the US-Brazil Energy Forum section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official U.S. government statement naming civil nuclear cooperation terms.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"Bolsonaro-era foreign and energy policy context, though negotiated via ministers.","contrary_evidence":["The statement covers civilian nuclear cooperation, not weapon-related choices."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}

13. ABACC’s 2022 news stream shows active safeguards training and confidence-building during Bolsonaro’s year.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"News – ABACC","publisher":"ABACC","publication_date":"2022-09-09; 2022-03-23; 2022-10-31","url":"https://www.abacc.org.br/en/news/","claim":"In 2022 ABACC reported a visit by CTBTO Executive Secretary Robert Floyd to exchange views on trust and confidence-building, and separate safeguards training for Brazil's Naval Agency for Nuclear Safety and Quality covering the legal basis of safeguards and inspection techniques.","locator":"news items dated 2022-09-09 and 2022-03-23","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official institutional news page with dated 2022 activities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional environment under Bolsonaro; not a personal Bolsonaro quote.","contrary_evidence":["This is institutional process evidence, not proof of personal presidential initiative."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.8","1B.10"]}

14. Brazil had only civil nuclear capacity, not a weapons arsenal, which keeps the chapter in limited-exposure territory.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country Nuclear Power Profiles - Brazil","publisher":"IAEA Power Reactor Information System","publication_date":"current profile with data as of 2026-07-08","url":"https://cnpp.iaea.org/public/countries/BR/profile/highlights","claim":"Brazil's profile shows two nuclear power reactors in operation and one under construction, with nuclear power supplying a small share of electricity.","locator":"country highlight panel","source_type":"primary/IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IAEA reactor and country-profile data.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Civil nuclear exposure context relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level nuclear infrastructure, not Bolsonaro personal control over weapons systems.","contrary_evidence":["Civil reactors are not the same as an existential-risk weapons capability."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}

15. Bolsonaro’s Moscow remarks in February 2022 show interest in civil nuclear reactors, but also a peaceful framing amid the Russia-Ukraine crisis.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bolsonaro says Brazil keen on Russian nuclear reactors, mum on Ukraine","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-02-17","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/02/17/us-russia-brazil-nuclear","claim":"During a Moscow visit, Bolsonaro said Brazil was interested in small nuclear reactors from Rosatom and said, 'We pray for peace,' while not mentioning Ukraine by name.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted sentence","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a contemporaneous diplomatic visit; the quote is directly attributed, but the overall framing is journalistic.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact pre-invasion 2022 timing, useful for rhetoric and civil-nuclear context.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Bolsonaro attribution for the Moscow trip and statement.","contrary_evidence":["The visit to Putin days before the invasion can be read as politically tone-deaf, even if not nuclear-brinkmanship."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.9"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**

I considered roughly 30 documents across these families: Planalto constitutional and nuclear-security texts, UN Treaty Collection status pages, IAEA legal-status and reactor-profile pages, ABACC institutional pages and 2022 news items, CTBTO treaty-status material, OPANAL treaty pages, U.S. Department of Energy bilateral-energy statements, and Reuters reporting carried by Euronews.

I rejected or deprioritized:
- Duplicate treaty-status pages that repeated the same treaty fact without adding new ruler-attributed evidence.
- Broad 2026 institutional pages on ANSN/ABACC that were useful for background but too retrospective for the 2022 ruler-period.
- Generic Bolsonaro political speeches and election coverage that did not speak to nuclear or existential-risk responsibility.
- Out-of-scope energy articles that discussed clean energy without a meaningful nuclear-risk or safeguards angle.
- Non-unique rewrites of the same 2022 ABACC news item.

**Remaining Gaps**

- There is still no direct evidence of Bolsonaro taking a distinctive, target-period nuclear-use, proliferation, or crisis-de-escalation decision, which is expected given Brazil’s non-weaponized posture.
- I did not find a 2022 ruler-specific nuclear-rhetoric incident beyond the Moscow reactor remarks; more searching is unlikely to change the overall exposure picture.
- I did not find evidence that Brazil advanced or rejected TPNW ratification in 2022 in a way that materially changes the chapter, beyond the existing signed-but-unratified status.
- If you want deeper sub-issue coverage, the only likely useful follow-up would be a narrower pass on ANSN appointment history or Bolsonaro-era civil nuclear procurement, not a broader search.

chapter_notes.2B:
Exposure classification: `limited_exposure_with_choices`

**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["2B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0002"],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0015"],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6"]}

**New evidence**

- Bolsonaro’s first major 2022 foreign-policy move on this file was the official Moscow visit, where Brazil and Russia discussed trade, energy, agriculture, defense, cybersecurity, science, and technology.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Jair Bolsonaro and President Vladimir Putin meet in Moscow","publisher":"Government of Brazil","publication_date":"2022-02-16","url":"https://www.gov.br/en/government-of-brazil/latest-news/2022/president-jair-bolsonaro-and-president-vladimir-putin-met-in-moscow","claim":"Bolsonaro met Putin on an official state visit to discuss strategic partnership and cooperation in trade, energy, agriculture, defense, cybersecurity, science and technology.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government release dated to the visit with direct attribution.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact February 2022 timing just before the invasion.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Bolsonaro attribution for the visit and discussion agenda.","contrary_evidence":["The release frames cooperation positively and does not itself show harmful conduct."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.8"]}

- Bolsonaro then publicly said Brazil would stay neutral and not take sides in the Russia-Ukraine war.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bolsonaro diz que não toma partido na invasão russa da Ucrânia","publisher":"UOL / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-30","url":"https://economia.uol.com.br/noticias/reuters/2022/03/30/bolsonaro-diz-que-nao-toma-partido-na-invasao-russa-da-ucrania.htm","claim":"Bolsonaro said Brazil would remain neutral in the Russia-Ukraine war and would not take sides, adding that the conflict was not Brazil's problem.","locator":"opening quote and second paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting republished by UOL, contemporaneous and directly attributed to Bolsonaro.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Bolsonaro quote.","contrary_evidence":["Neutrality can be read as restraint, but it also avoided an explicit condemnation of the invasion."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6"]}

- Brazil’s UN delegation said the Security Council should pursue diplomatic solutions and expressed grave concern over Russian military operations in Ukraine.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement and Explanation of Vote by the Permanent Representative Ambassador Ronaldo Costa Filho in the Security Council Debate on the Question of Ukraine","publisher":"Ministério das Relações Exteriores","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.gov.br/mre/en/contact-us/press-area/press-releases/statement-and-explanation-of-vote-by-the-permanent-representative-ambassador-ronaldo-costa-filho-in-the-security-council-debate-on-the-question-of-ukraine","claim":"Brazil said the Security Council should pursue diplomatic solutions to the Ukraine crisis and expressed grave concern about Russian military operations against Ukrainian territory.","locator":"opening statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official foreign-ministry statement of vote and rationale from Brazil's UN mission.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact February 2022 crisis response.","ruler_attribution":"Official Brazilian foreign-policy position under Bolsonaro.","contrary_evidence":["This is delegation-level language, so it is not a personal Bolsonaro speech."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.7"]}

- Brazil supported Ukraine’s request for a Human Rights Council emergency session right after the invasion.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Brazil voted to support Ukraine's request for a Human Rights Council emergency","publisher":"Government of Brazil","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://www.gov.br/en/government-of-brazil/latest-news/2022/brazil-voted-to-support-ukraine2019s-request-for-a-human-rights-council-emergency","claim":"Brazil voted to support Ukraine's request for a Human Rights Council emergency session on the Ukrainian conflict.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government summary of Brazil's vote at the UN.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 crisis period.","ruler_attribution":"Official Brazilian vote under Bolsonaro's government.","contrary_evidence":["Support for an emergency session is procedural and does not by itself settle Brazil's broader war stance."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- Brazil also voted to condemn Russia’s aggression and demand withdrawal from Ukraine.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Brazil votes to condemn Russia's aggression against Ukraine at the UN General Assembly","publisher":"Government of Brazil","publication_date":"2022-03-02","url":"https://www.gov.br/en/government-of-brazil/latest-news/2022/brazil-votes-to-condemn-russia2019s-aggression-against-ukraine-at-the-un-general-assembly/","claim":"Brazil voted at the UN General Assembly to condemn Russia's aggression against Ukraine and demand that Moscow stop fighting and withdraw its military forces.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government summary of Brazil's vote and rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact March 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Official Brazilian foreign-policy action under Bolsonaro.","contrary_evidence":["The same government also continued to stress neutrality and dialogue in other forums."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Brazil created a humanitarian visa channel for Ukrainians and stateless people affected by the war by direct presidential determination.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nota conjunta MJSP E MRE","publisher":"Ministério da Justiça e Segurança Pública","publication_date":"2022-03-03","url":"https://www.gov.br/mj/pt-br/assuntos/noticias/nota-conjunta-mjsp-e-mre-1","claim":"At President Jair Bolsonaro's determination, Brazil established temporary visas and residence authorization for Ukrainians and stateless persons affected or displaced by the conflict in Ukraine.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministerial notice explicitly attributes the measure to Bolsonaro.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Bolsonaro attribution through the ministerial note.","contrary_evidence":["This is humanitarian protection, not a statement about the war's legality or strategy."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Brazil later voted for a UN resolution focused on humanitarian access and protection of civilians in Ukraine.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Brazil voted in favor of the resolution Humanitarian consequences of the aggression against Ukraine","publisher":"Government of Brazil","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://www.gov.br/en/government-of-brazil/latest-news/2022/brazil-voted-in-favor-of-the-resolution-201chumanitarian-consequences-of-the-aggression-against-ukraine201d","claim":"Brazil voted for a resolution demanding humanitarian access to Ukraine and a halt to attacks on civilians, medical personnel, civilian buildings, and infrastructure.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government summary of the UN vote and text focus.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Official Brazilian vote under Bolsonaro's government.","contrary_evidence":["The vote was humanitarian, not a full strategic endorsement of stronger coercive measures."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- Brazil did support an HRC commission of inquiry on Ukraine, but abstained on the stronger Russia-suspension resolution because it wanted the inquiry to finish independently first.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"General Assembly Adopts Text to Suspend Russian Federation from Human Rights Council, Continuing Emergency Special Session on Humanitarian Crisis in Ukraine","publisher":"United Nations Press","publication_date":"2022-04-07","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12414.doc.htm","claim":"Brazil said it had voted for the Human Rights Council commission of inquiry on Ukraine, but abstained on the General Assembly suspension vote because it wanted that independent investigation to complete first.","locator":"Brazil explanation of vote section","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN meeting record with Brazil's explanation of vote.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Brazil's UN delegation under Bolsonaro's government.","contrary_evidence":["Abstention can be read as procedural caution rather than full solidarity with accountability efforts."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- Brazil’s UN speech in June stressed accountability for sexual violence and human trafficking and said humanitarian visas were being granted to Ukrainians.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by the Permanent Representative of Brazil to the United Nations, Ambassador Ronaldo Costa Filho, at the UNSC meeting on Ukraine - June 6th, 2022","publisher":"Ministério das Relações Exteriores","publication_date":"2024-06-21","url":"https://www.gov.br/mre/pt-br/delbrasonu/the-permanent-mission-of-brazil-to-the-un/statements/2022/june-2022/statement-by-the-permanent-representative-of-brazil-to-the-united-nations-ambassador-ronaldo-costa-filho-at-the-unsc-meeting-on-ukraine-june-6th-2022","claim":"Brazil called for accountability for sexual violence and human trafficking in Ukraine and said special humanitarian visas were being granted to Ukrainians affected by the conflict.","locator":"middle paragraphs on accountability and humanitarian visas","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official foreign-ministry archive reproducing the 2022 statement text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 statement archived later on the ministry site.","ruler_attribution":"Brazilian foreign-policy position under Bolsonaro; not a personal Bolsonaro quote.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a mission statement, so it reflects official diplomacy more than direct presidential command."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- Brazil held the rotating presidency of the Security Council in July 2022, with Ukraine among the agenda items.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Presidência brasileira do Conselho de Segurança das Nações Unidas","publisher":"Ministério das Relações Exteriores","publication_date":"2022-07-01","url":"https://www.gov.br/mre/pt-br/canais_atendimento/imprensa/notas-a-imprensa/presidencia-brasileira-do-conselho-de-seguranca-das-nacoes-unidas","claim":"Brazil assumed the monthly presidency of the UN Security Council in July 2022 and the Council agenda included the Ukraine situation.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official notice on Brazil's Council presidency and agenda.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional role of the Brazilian government during Bolsonaro's term.","contrary_evidence":["A rotating presidency is procedural power, not proof of substantive peace leadership."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

- Brazil’s permanent representative later described the September annexation referenda as an escalation and called for an immediate ceasefire and peace negotiations.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by the Permanent Representative of Brazil to the United Nations, Ambassador Ronaldo Costa Filho, at the UNSC meeting on Ukraine - September 27th, 2022","publisher":"Ministério das Relações Exteriores","publication_date":"2024-06-21","url":"https://www.gov.br/mre/pt-br/delbrasonu/the-permanent-mission-of-brazil-to-the-un/statements/2022/september-2022/statement-by-the-permanent-representative-of-brazil-to-the-united-nations-ambassador-ronaldo-costa-filho-at-the-unsc-meeting-on-ukraine-september-27th-2022","claim":"Brazil said the referenda in occupied Ukrainian regions escalated the conflict and called for an immediate ceasefire and the opening of peace negotiations.","locator":"final third of the statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official archived statement with explicit ceasefire language and dated 2022 text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 statement archived later.","ruler_attribution":"Brazilian foreign-policy position under Bolsonaro's government.","contrary_evidence":["The statement also defended self-determination language, which can soften the apparent alignment with one side."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- When the General Assembly voted on the annexation resolution in October, Brazil abstained because it wanted dialogue and said nuclear use would be catastrophic.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"With 143 Votes in Favour, 5 Against, General Assembly Adopts Resolution Condemning Russian Federation’s Annexation of Four Eastern Ukraine Regions","publisher":"United Nations Press","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12458.doc.htm","claim":"Brazil abstained on the annexation resolution, saying its calls for a cessation of hostilities and return to dialogue were not included and warning that any use of nuclear weapons would be catastrophic for humanity.","locator":"Brazil explanation of vote section","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN meeting record with Brazil's stated rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Brazil's UN delegation under Bolsonaro's government.","contrary_evidence":["Abstention on a condemnation resolution can be read as weaker accountability than a yes vote."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.7","2B.9"]}

- Brazil again abstained in November on the reparations register because it said the text did not foster constructive dialogue and lacked proper UN supervision.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"General Assembly Adopts Text Recommending Creation of Register to Document Damages Caused by Russian Federation Aggression against Ukraine, Resuming Emergency Special Session","publisher":"United Nations Press","publication_date":"2022-11-14","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12470.doc.htm","claim":"Brazil abstained on the reparations resolution because it said the text did not foster constructive dialogue and created uncertainty outside UN supervision.","locator":"Brazil explanation of vote section","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN meeting record with Brazil's stated rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fit.","ruler_attribution":"Brazil's UN delegation under Bolsonaro's government.","contrary_evidence":["The abstention was consistent with Brazil's dialogue-first posture, but it also limited support for accountability.","],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**

I reviewed roughly 30 documents across official Brazilian government releases, UN press records, Reuters-wrapped reporting, and a smaller set of secondary explainers. The retained set is deliberately narrow: 13 new URLs plus the reused baseline items above.

I rejected or deprioritized:
- Duplicate Reuters rewrites of the same neutrality quote that added no new facts.
- Secondary explainers that only paraphrased Reuters or UN records without adding ruler-specific attribution.
- Domestic-security items such as the decree on military support for election administration, because they are domestic and belong outside 2B.
- Broad background pages on Ukraine, Russia, or Brazil-Russia trade that did not materially change the peace/aggression analysis.
- Retrospective pages that were useful only as background and did not add 2022 conduct.
- Repeated coverage of the same UN vote where the underlying claim was already captured by an official Brazil or UN source.

**Remaining gaps**

- There is still no evidence that Bolsonaro directly controlled any foreign military campaign, proxy force, or arms recipient in 2022. That is expected for Brazil, but it limits the ceiling on chapter confidence.
- The strongest chapter-relevant conduct is diplomatic and multilateral, not battlefield command. More search is unlikely to change that basic exposure picture.
- The main unresolved nuance is whether Bolsonaro’s Moscow visit and neutrality rhetoric should be read as mere hedging or as materially weak condemnation of Russian aggression. More Bolsonaro-only speech search could sharpen that, but probably not alter the overall record.
- If you want a tighter attribution pass, the next useful search would be Bolsonaro's own 2022 speeches and Itamaraty releases, not broader Ukraine background.

chapter_notes.3B:
Exposure classification: `recurring_abuse_and_intimidation`, not mass terror. The 2022 record is mainly about Bolsonaro’s campaign rhetoric, attacks on democratic institutions and journalists, permissive gun policy, and weakened protection for Indigenous and other vulnerable groups. Some protection remained through courts and elections, but the overall pattern is materially negative.

